This came up on March 18, 2024 (block 2), when you asked me to help refactor your `PlayerManager` class because it mixed network logic with game state updates (addPlayer/updatePlayer/removePlayer each contained "network logic to send player data to clients" inline).

**How I recommended restructuring it:**

1. **Create a `NetworkManager` class** — a new class whose sole job is handling all network-related tasks.
2. **Strip the network logic out of `PlayerManager`** and delegate it to the `NetworkManager` instead, so `PlayerManager` only manages the game state (the `players` map).

Concretely, the `PlayerManager` constructor was changed to accept a `NetworkManager` instance (dependency injection):

```typescript
class PlayerManager {
  private players: { [id: string]: Player };
  private networkManager: NetworkManager;

  constructor(networkManager: NetworkManager) {
    this.players = {};
    this.networkManager = networkManager;
  }

  public addPlayer(player: Player) {
    this.players[player.id] = player;
    this.networkManager.sendPlayerData(player);
  }
  // ...same delegation pattern for updatePlayer and removePlayer
}
```

**The role I assigned to the new component (`NetworkManager`):**

It became the single owner of all network communication, exposing three methods that map exactly to the state changes:
- `sendPlayerData(player)` — sending new player data to clients
- `sendUpdatedPlayerData(player)` — sending updated player data
- `sendPlayerRemovalData(playerId)` — sending player removal data

I explained the benefits: the `PlayerManager` constructor taking a `NetworkManager` instance allows easy testing and flexibility, and separating the concerns makes the code more modular and maintainable — you can modify or replace the network logic without touching game state management.

**Related follow-up:** Later in the same block (your message #278, answered in #279), when you asked how to keep networking and game logic separate at the module level without over-complicating things, I extended this into a three-module structure: a **Networking Module** (connections, sending/receiving), a **Game Logic Module** (player movement, collision detection, AI), and a **Message Handling Module**, whose role was to act as the intermediary between the other two — translating network messages into game events and vice versa — with a suggested `src/networking/`, `src/gameLogic/`, `src/messageHandling/` directory layout.

